CHUNK({"chunk":"4b","module":"m4","topics":[{"id":"m4-welding-basics","title":"Welding and the weld zones","short":"Welding basics","blurb":"What welding is and the three regions of a welded butt joint: base metal, fusion zone and heat affected zone.","source":"Lecture 12, pages 40–41","tabs":[{"title":"Definition","html":"<p class=\"lead\">According to the American Welding Society (AWS), <b>welding</b> is a materials joining process that produces <b>coalescence</b> (merging into a single, unified piece) by heating the materials to suitable temperatures.</p>\n<ul><li>It may be done <b>with or without pressure</b>, or by pressure alone.</li><li>It may be done <b>with or without filler material</b>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Weld zones","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A cross section through a welded butt joint shows three regions.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m4-weld-zones\"></figure>\n<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Base metal (BM)</span><b>The parent plates, unaffected</b></div><div><span>Fusion zone (FZ)</span><b>Filler metal is deposited here; the properties depend mainly on the filler metal</b></div><div><span>Heat affected zone (HAZ)</span><b>Microstructure and properties are altered. Weaker than both the BM and the FZ because of softening</b></div></div>\n<div class=\"note trap\"><p>The HAZ is the weakest region, not the weld itself. It is the narrow area immediately surrounding the welded base metal.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>On pipe root welds a blue colour appears from oxidation at about 316 °C (600 °F). That blue band does <b>not</b> show the HAZ width.</p></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is welding (AWS definition)?","back":"A materials joining process that produces coalescence by heating to suitable temperatures, with or without pressure, or by pressure alone, and with or without filler material."},{"front":"What is coalescence?","back":"Merging of materials to form a single, unified piece."},{"front":"Name the three regions of a welded butt joint.","back":"Base metal (BM), fusion zone (FZ) and heat affected zone (HAZ)."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-welding-classes","title":"Classification of welding processes","short":"Welding classification","blurb":"The four groups of commercial welding processes and the processes in each, plus the energy-density ranking.","source":"Lecture 12, pages 42–44, 62–64","tabs":[{"title":"Four groups","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Commercial welding processes fall into four groups.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Processes</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Gas welding</td><td>Oxyacetylene welding</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electric arc welding</td><td>SMAW (shielded metal arc), GTAW (gas tungsten arc), GMAW (gas metal arc), PAW (plasma arc), FCAW (flux cored arc), SAW (submerged arc), ESW (electroslag)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Power beam (high density) welding</td><td>Electron beam welding (EBW), laser beam welding (LBW)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Solid state welding</td><td>Friction stir welding (FSW), friction welding (FW), resistance welding (RSW)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<div class=\"note exam\"><p>Learn the abbreviations with the groups. Arc welding is the biggest group and the one examined most.</p></div>"},{"title":"Heat source power","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Higher energy densities <b>reduce heat losses</b> and give greater arc efficiency.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m4-power-density\"></figure>\n<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Gas welding</span><b>About 10 W/cm², about 3300 °C. Oxyacetylene</b></div><div><span>Arc welding</span><b>About 50 W/cm², about 6000 °C. GTAW, GMAW, SMAW, FCAW, PAW, SAW</b></div><div><span>High energy beam</span><b>About 10000 W/cm², about 20000 to 30000 °C. LBW, EBW</b></div></div>\n<p>Moving from gas to beam welding, <b>penetration, welding speed, weld quality and equipment cost</b> all increase. Moving the other way, the heat input to the workpiece rises, and so does damage to it.</p>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name the four groups of commercial welding processes.","back":"Gas welding, electric arc welding, power beam (high density) welding, solid state welding."},{"front":"Which processes are power beam welding?","back":"Electron beam welding (EBW) and laser beam welding (LBW)."},{"front":"Which processes are solid state welding?","back":"Friction stir welding (FSW), friction welding (FW) and resistance welding (RSW)."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-arc-welding","title":"Arc welding: polarity and parameters","short":"Arc polarity, parameters","blurb":"DCEN, DCEP and AC polarity, and how current, arc voltage, welding speed and filler diameter change the weld.","source":"Lecture 12, pages 45–49","tabs":[{"title":"Polarity","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Three polarities are used in electric arc welding.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Polarity</th><th>Heat</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>DCEN (straight polarity)</td><td>About two-thirds of the power at the work end of the arc, one-third at the electrode</td><td>Narrow and deep weld</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DCEP (reverse polarity)</td><td>Heating effect of the electrons is now at the tungsten electrode</td><td>Shallow weld; used on thin sheets of strong oxide-forming materials</td></tr>\n<tr><td>AC</td><td>Alternates between the two</td><td>Good penetration and oxide cleaning action together</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>In DCEP the positive ions of the shielding gas bombard the workpiece, knocking off the oxide film and leaving a <b>clean weld surface</b>.</p>"},{"title":"Welding current","html":"<p class=\"lead\">More current gives more <b>penetration</b> into the fusion zone.</p>\n<p>Current is the main control for how deep the weld goes.</p>"},{"title":"Arc voltage and speed","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Arc voltage follows <b>arc length</b>. If the arc length changes too far either way, the arc becomes <b>unstable</b>.</p>\n<p>Welding speed works the opposite way to penetration:</p>\n<ul><li>Faster welding gives <b>less depth of penetration</b>.</li><li>It also gives high productivity, <b>less heat input</b>, less distortion and less residual stress.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Filler diameter","html":"<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Larger electrode</h4><p>Current has to be increased. Gives a <b>high deposition rate</b>.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Smaller electrode</h4><p>Less deposition rate. A smaller diameter means <b>higher current density</b>.</p></div></div>"},{"title":"SMAW process","html":"<p class=\"lead\"><b>Shielded metal arc welding (SMAW)</b>, also called manual metal arc or stick welding, uses a <b>consumable electrode coated with flux</b>; an electric arc between the electrode and the work melts both.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m4-smaw\"></figure>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>The power source (AC transformer or DC rectifier) is connected to the electrode holder and to the workpiece.</li>\n<li>The welder strikes the arc by touching the electrode on the work and lifting it slightly. The arc (about 5000 °C) melts the tip of the core wire and the base metal, forming a <b>weld pool</b>.</li>\n<li>The <b>flux coating</b> burns to give a <b>shielding gas</b> that keeps oxygen and nitrogen of the air away from the molten metal, and melts into <b>slag</b> that floats on the pool and protects the bead as it cools.</li>\n<li>The electrode is moved along the joint and fed down as it is consumed; after cooling the slag is chipped off.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Advantages</h4><p>Simple, cheap, portable equipment; works outdoors and in all positions; welds most steels and cast iron.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Limitations</h4><p>Slow (electrode must be changed, slag removed); needs a skilled welder; not suited to thin sheet or reactive metals.</p></div></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is DCEN?","back":"Straight polarity: about two-thirds of the power is at the work end, giving a narrow, deep weld."},{"front":"What is DCEP?","back":"Reverse polarity: heating at the tungsten electrode, shallow weld, used on thin sheets of oxide-forming materials."},{"front":"Why does DCEP clean the weld surface?","back":"Positive ions of the shielding gas bombard the workpiece and knock off oxide films."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-weld-joints","title":"Weld joints and positions","short":"Joints and positions","blurb":"The basic joint types, butt weld groove shapes by thickness, the four welding positions and joint preparation rules.","source":"Lecture 12, pages 50–54","tabs":[{"title":"Joint types","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Five basic weld joints.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m4-weld-joints\"></figure>\n<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Butt joint</span><b>Two plates end to end in one plane</b></div><div><span>Lap joint</span><b>Plates overlap</b></div><div><span>T-joint</span><b>One plate joins the other at a right angle, forming a T</b></div><div><span>Edge joint</span><b>Used in sheet metal fabrication, particularly when the thickness is less than 3 mm. Found in tanks, boxes and metal enclosures</b></div><div><span>Corner joint</span><b>Connects two pieces at a right angle, forming an L-shape. Found in sheet metal work, box construction and frame manufacturing</b></div></div>"},{"title":"Butt weld grooves","html":"<p class=\"lead\">The groove shape depends on the plate thickness.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Joint</th><th>Plate thickness</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Square butt</td><td>Less than 5 mm; no bevelling needed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Single V or single U</td><td>5 mm to 12.5 mm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Double V or double U</td><td>Greater than 12.5 mm</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>"},{"title":"Welding positions","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Four positions are shown: <b>1G, 2G, 3G and 4G</b>, where G stands for groove weld.</p>\n<p><b>Extra:</b> the standard names are 1G flat, 2G horizontal, 3G vertical and 4G overhead.</p>"},{"title":"Joint preparation","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Standard guidelines for weld joint preparation:</p>\n<ul><li>Make the joint with the <b>least amount of weld metal</b>. Over-welding causes distortion and raises fabrication time and cost, and does not improve strength or performance.</li><li>Use a <b>double V or U groove</b> on thick plates to cut weld metal and control distortion.</li><li>Use an <b>optimum root opening and included angle</b> to reduce the filler metal needed.</li><li><b>Avoid joints</b> that make extremely deep grooves.</li></ul>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name the basic weld joints.","back":"Butt, lap, T, edge and corner joints."},{"front":"Where are edge joints used?","back":"Sheet metal fabrication, particularly under 3 mm thickness: tanks, boxes, metal enclosures."},{"front":"What does a corner joint form?","back":"An L-shape, joining two pieces at a right angle; used in box construction and frames."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-gtaw-gmaw","title":"GTAW (TIG) and GMAW (MIG)","short":"TIG and MIG","blurb":"Two clean gas-shielded arc processes, how each works and how they compare on strength, speed, gas, looks, difficulty and cost.","source":"Lecture 12, pages 55–61","tabs":[{"title":"GTAW (TIG)","html":"<p class=\"lead\"><b>Gas tungsten arc welding</b>, formerly <b>tungsten inert gas (TIG)</b>, is an <b>inert gas shielded</b> arc process with a <b>non-consumable</b> tungsten electrode (with 1–2% thorium oxide mixed in).</p>\n<ul><li>Most used for <b>aluminium alloys, magnesium alloys and stainless steel</b>.</li><li>The DC supply can be steady or <b>pulsed</b>. In pulsed mode the current has two levels: melting happens at <b>peak current</b>; at the low level the arc is kept but very little heat goes in, so the weld metal cools.</li><li><b>Low deposition rate and slow speed</b> give clean joints with better properties.</li></ul>"},{"title":"GMAW (MIG)","html":"<p class=\"lead\"><b>Gas metal arc welding</b>, or <b>metal inert gas (MIG)</b>, uses a <b>consumable</b> electrode.</p>\n<ul><li>Also a clean process, with a <b>higher deposition rate</b> than GTAW and so higher productivity.</li><li>Generally the <b>root passes</b> are done with TIG and the <b>fill passes</b> with MIG.</li></ul>"},{"title":"TIG vs MIG","html":"<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>TIG</th><th>MIG</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Strength</td><td>Typically stronger: narrow, focused arc gives better penetration and fewer defects</td><td>Less</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed</td><td>Slower</td><td>Faster: filler fed automatically, rounder and broader arc dissipates heat, longer runs without overheating</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shielding gas</td><td>Pure argon (tungsten is sensitive to oxygen or CO<sub>2</sub>)</td><td>Argon and CO<sub>2</sub>, typically 75/25%; CO<sub>2</sub> stabilises the arc and aids penetration</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Looks</td><td>Better; little or no spatter, only light polishing</td><td>More spatter</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Difficulty</td><td>Harder: two hands, one for the torch and one for the filler rod</td><td>Much easier to learn</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost</td><td>More per foot of bead: low deposition and more experienced welders</td><td>Less</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is GTAW and its old name?","back":"Gas tungsten arc welding, formerly TIG (tungsten inert gas)."},{"front":"Is the TIG electrode consumable?","back":"No. It is a tungsten electrode with 1–2% thorium oxide."},{"front":"What metals is TIG mostly used on?","back":"Aluminium alloys, magnesium alloys and stainless steel."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[{"id":"m4-smaw","src":"own/README.txt","crop":null,"caption":"SMAW: arc between the flux-coated electrode and the work, with shielding gas, weld pool, slag and bead.","alt":"Inclined coated electrode in a holder over a workpiece, arc, gas shield, molten pool, slag over the bead and power source leads."},{"id":"m4-weld-zones","src":"m4/pages/p40.png","crop":null,"caption":"Cross section of a welded butt joint: base metal on each side, the fusion zone in the middle and the heat affected zone between them.","alt":"Butt weld cross section showing base metal, fusion zone and heat affected zone."},{"id":"m4-weld-joints","src":"m4/pages/p50.png","crop":null,"caption":"The five basic weld joints: butt, lap, T, edge and corner.","alt":"Five small sketches of weld joint types."},{"id":"m4-power-density","src":"m4/pages/p63.png","crop":null,"caption":"Heat source power density against heat input: gas, arc and high energy beam welding.","alt":"Curve of power density with gas, arc and beam welding regions."}]});
